.TH CUP 1 "2024" "calculinux-update" "Calculinux System Updates"
.SH NAME
cup, cup-hook, cup-postreboot \- Calculinux system update tools
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cup
.RB [ list | download | install | status | reconcile ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B cup-hook
.I hook_type slot_name
.br
.B cup-postreboot
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Calculinux update system provides A/B slot\-based system updates with automatic OPKG package reconciliation. It consists of three commands:
.TP
.B cup
Main command\-line interface for browsing, downloading, and installing RAUC update bundles with integrated package prefetching.
.TP
.B cup\-hook
RAUC post\-install handler invoked during bundle installation to perform complete package reconciliation planning. Removes duplicate packages, prunes writable status, and queues pending operations before reboot.
.TP
.B cup\-postreboot
Post\-reboot service that carries out the package work queued by cup\-hook: removing overlay copies of packages the new image now provides, and reinstalling overlay packages when the release or kernel changed. It never needs the network; reinstalls come from the prefetch cache and anything else waits for
.BR "cup reconcile" .
.SS "Bundle Extras"
Calculinux RAUC bundles include additional metadata beyond the root filesystem image. The
.B bundle\-extras.tar.gz
file embedded in each bundle contains:
.PP
.nf
.RS
extras/opkg/etc/opkg/    # OPKG configuration from new image
extras/opkg/status.image # Package status from new image
.RE
.fi
.PP
This metadata serves two purposes:
.IP 1. 3
Enables the prefetch system to download packages before installation
.IP 2.
Provides status.image to cup\-hook without requiring slot mounting
.PP
Bundle extras are extracted by the RAUC post\-install handler and passed to cup\-hook via the
.B RAUC_BUNDLE_STATUS_IMAGE
environment variable. This allows cup\-hook to perform complete reconciliation without mounting the newly installed slot, improving reliability and performance.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "cup list"
List available update bundles from configured channels.
.PP
.B cup list
.RI [ --channel
.IR CHANNEL ]
.PP
Lists bundles sorted by date (newest first). If no channel is specified and multiple channels are enabled, lists bundles from all channels.
.SS "cup download"
Download an update bundle without installing it.
.PP
.B cup download
.RB [ --channel
.IR CHANNEL ]
.B --bundle
.I BUNDLE
.RI [ --destination
.IR DIR ]
.PP
Downloads the specified bundle to the cache directory (default: /var/cache/calculinux\-update/) for later installation.
.SS "cup install"
Install a system update bundle.
.PP
.B cup install
.RI [ --channel
.IR CHANNEL ]
.RI [ --bundle
.IR BUNDLE ]
.RI [ --yes ]
.RI [ --dry-run ]
.RI [ --no-prefetch ]
.RI [ --force ]
.PP
Interactively select and install an update bundle. The installation process:
.PP
1. Checks the bundle's minimum Calculinux version (from the mirror index and from the bundle itself)
.br
2. Works out which overlay packages must be reinstalled for the new image (all of them for a new release, the kernel modules for a new kernel, none otherwise)
.br
3. Downloads those packages and their dependencies while the system is still online, and asks before continuing if some cannot be fetched
.br
4. Invokes RAUC to install the bundle to inactive slot
.br
5. System reboot required to complete the update
.PP
After reboot, the
.B cup\-postreboot
service finishes the package work from the prefetch cache without needing the network.
.SS "cup status"
Show package work left over from the last update: packages still waiting to be reinstalled, and packages that could not be reinstalled.
.SS "cup reconcile"
Finish the package work an update queued, downloading from the feed as needed. Run it after connecting to the network when
.B cup status
or the login message lists waiting packages;
.B cup\-reconcile.timer
also retries every 15 minutes. Exits 2 while packages are still waiting for the network.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-channel " " \fICHANNEL\fR
Specify which update channel to use. Must match a channel name from
.IR /etc/calculinux-update.toml .
If not specified and multiple channels are enabled, prompts for selection.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bundle " " \fIBUNDLE\fR
Specify which bundle to download or install. If not specified for install command, prompts for selection from available bundles.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Skip confirmation prompts. Useful for automation. Automatically proceeds with installation without user confirmation.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-destination " " \fIDIR\fR
Specify download destination directory. Only applies to download command. Defaults to /var/cache/calculinux\-update/.
.TP
.BR \-\-dry\-run
Perform all steps except the actual RAUC installation. Downloads bundle and performs prefetch but stops before invoking RAUC. Useful for testing.
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-prefetch
Skip package prefetch phase. Bundle extras will still be extracted for validation, but packages will not be pre\-downloaded. Reinstalls needed after the update then wait for
.BR "cup reconcile" .
.TP
.BR \-\-force
Install even if the bundle requires a newer running Calculinux version. Also allows the bundle's install\-check hook to accept the install (via
.IR /run/calculinux\-update/allow\-install\-check\-override ).
.SH CONFIGURATION
.SS "Configuration File"
.I /etc/calculinux\-update.toml
.PP
TOML configuration file defining update channels and mirror settings.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.nf
.RS
mirror_base_url = "https://mirror.example.com"

[[channels]]
name = "Release"
path = "/update/walnascar/release"
enable = true

[[channels]]
name = "Continuous"
path = "/update/walnascar/continuous"
enable = false
.RE
.fi
.SS "Channel Configuration"
Each channel defines:
.TP
.B name
Display name shown in
.BR "cup list" .
.TP
.B path
URL path appended to mirror_base_url to access bundle list.
.TP
.B enable
Whether channel appears in interactive listings. Set to
.B false
to hide development channels.
.SS "Mirror Structure"
Update mirrors must provide a
.I bundles.txt
file at each channel path with the format:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# timestamp filename size sha256
1732147200 calculinux\-bundle\-20241120.raucb 123456789 abc123...
.RE
.fi
.SH PACKAGE RECONCILIATION
.SS "The Problem"
Calculinux uses dual\-layer package management:
.TP
.B Base layer
Read\-only packages baked into the RAUC image (
.IR /var/lib/opkg/status.image
).
.TP
.B Overlay layer
User\-installed packages in writable storage (
.IR /var/lib/opkg/status
).
.PP
When updating the base image, three problems arise:
.TP
1.
.B Shadowing
\- User\-installed packages that now exist in the new base (duplicates).
.TP
2.
.B Missing packages
\- Packages removed from new base that user packages depend on.
.TP
3.
.B Version conflicts
\- Overlay packages compiled against old base versions.
.SS "Reconciliation Phases"
The update system solves these problems in two phases:
.TP
.B Phase 1: Prefetch (cup install, before installation)
When the new image changes the release (codename or Yocto series) or the kernel, overlay packages must be reinstalled from the new feeds.
.B cup install
resolves them and their dependencies against the new image and downloads them into
.IR /var/cache/calculinux\-update/prefetch/ ,
keeping the feed lists in
.IR /var/lib/calculinux\-update/prefetch\-lists/ ,
so the reinstall needs no network after the reboot.
.TP
.B Phase 2: Hook (during RAUC installation)
.B cup\-hook
is invoked by RAUC's post\-install handler with the new image's
.I status.image
(via
.BR RAUC_BUNDLE_STATUS_IMAGE ).
It sorts every entry of the writable status file:
.RS
.IP 1. 3
.B Duplicates
(also in the new image): if files of the package are in the upper layer, the overlay copy is queued for removal after reboot; otherwise only its status entry is dropped now.
.IP 2.
.B Leaked image entries
(no files in the upper layer, and either in the current image or without any
.I .list
file): copies of image packages opkg wrote to the writable status file. Their entries are dropped.
.IP 3.
.B Overlay packages
(what the user installed): kept. Queued for reinstall only for a new release, or, for a new kernel, when they are kernel modules.
.RE
.IP
Upper\-layer state comes from the overlayfs
.B OVL_IOC_UPPER_STATE
ioctl. The hook's compatibility check only logs: RAUC has already marked the slot active when the handler runs; the bundle's install\-check hook is what refuses an install.
.TP
.B Phase 3: Post\-reboot (after slot switch)
.B cup\-postreboot
runs on the first boot into the new slot, without waiting for the network:
.RS
.IP 1. 3
Removes the queued overlay duplicates with one
.BR "opkg remove" ,
then removes the whiteouts left over the image's files and metadata
.IP 2.
Reinstalls queued packages from the prefetch cache when it was made for the running image
.IP 3.
Otherwise tries the feed; without network the packages stay queued for
.B cup reconcile
(retried by
.BR cup\-reconcile.timer ),
and a login message says how to finish
.IP 4.
Packages the feed no longer provides are recorded as leftovers (see
.BR "cup status" )
.RE
.TP
.B Phase 4: Config File Detection (during RAUC installation)
After package reconciliation planning,
.B cup\-hook
detects modified configuration files that may shadow new versions from the updated image:
.RS
.IP 1. 3
Reads CONFFILES metadata from
.I /var/lib/opkg/info/<package>.conffiles
for all packages in the new base image
.IP 2.
For each config file, compares MD5 checksums between upper layer (user modifications) and lower layer (new image version)
.IP 3.
If checksums differ, the user has modified the config file and it shadows the new version
.IP 4.
Copies the new version from lower layer to
.I <path>.dpkg\-new
in the upper layer
.IP 5.
Saves list of modified config files to
.I /var/lib/calculinux\-update/update\-state.modified\-conffiles
for post\-reboot reporting
.RE
.IP
After reboot,
.B cup\-postreboot
reports modified config files to the user, allowing manual review and merging of changes. The
.I .dpkg\-new
files contain the new version from the updated image, while the original paths contain the user's modifications.
.SS "Config File Handling"
.TP
.B The Problem
When updating the base image with RAUC:
.RS
.IP 1. 3
Config files in the lower layer (base image) are replaced with new versions
.IP 2.
User\-modified copies in the upper layer (OverlayFS) persist unchanged
.IP 3.
Upper layer modifications shadow the new lower layer versions
.IP 4.
User never sees new config options, security fixes, or format changes
.IP 5.
OPKG's CONFFILES mechanism only works during package upgrades, not image replacements
.RE
.TP
.B The Solution
The reconciliation system automatically detects config file conflicts:
.RS
.IP 1. 3
Reads CONFFILES metadata for all packages in new base image
.IP 2.
For each config file, accesses both layers of OverlayFS directly:
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
Upper layer:
.I /data/overlay/<dir>/upper/<path>
(user's version)
.IP \(bu
Lower layer:
.I /data/overlay/<dir>/lower/<path>
(new image version)
.RE
.IP 3.
Computes MD5 checksums of both versions
.IP 4.
If different, user has modified the file and it needs manual review
.IP 5.
Copies new version to
.I <path>.dpkg\-new
in upper layer
.IP 6.
After reboot, reports files requiring manual merge
.RE
.TP
.B User Workflow
After a RAUC update with modified config files, the user sees:
.PP
.nf
.RS
Modified config files detected (user changes shadow new image versions):
Package: openssh\-server
  /etc/ssh/sshd_config \-> /etc/ssh/sshd_config.dpkg\-new
Package: network\-manager
  /etc/network/interfaces \-> /etc/network/interfaces.dpkg\-new
.RE
.fi
.PP
To review and merge changes:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# Compare files
diff /etc/ssh/sshd_config /etc/ssh/sshd_config.dpkg\-new

# Or use interactive merge tool
vimdiff /etc/ssh/sshd_config /etc/ssh/sshd_config.dpkg\-new

# After merging, keep user version:
# (no action needed, .dpkg\-new file remains for reference)

# Or replace with new version:
mv /etc/ssh/sshd_config.dpkg\-new /etc/ssh/sshd_config

# Or merge manually:
# 1. Edit config file to add new options
# 2. Remove .dpkg\-new file when satisfied
rm /etc/ssh/sshd_config.dpkg\-new
.RE
.fi
.PP
The
.I .dpkg\-new
files are
.B not
automatically removed, allowing users to review them at their convenience.
.TP
.B CONFFILES Format
OPKG packages declare config files in
.I /var/lib/opkg/info/<package>.conffiles
with format:
.PP
.nf
.RS
/path/to/config [md5sum]
.RE
.fi
.PP
Each line specifies:
.IP \(bu 3
Absolute path to config file
.IP \(bu
Optional MD5 checksum (newer OPKG versions)
.IP \(bu
Lines starting with
.B #
are comments and ignored
.PP
Example:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# SSH daemon configuration
/etc/ssh/sshd_config d41d8cd98f00b204e9800998ecf8427e
/etc/ssh/ssh_config
/etc/default/ssh
.RE
.fi
.SH WHITEOUT CLEANUP
Calculinux uses OverlayFS to provide a writable layer on top of the read\-only base image. Whiteout cleanup addresses two specific edge cases:
.SS "Package File Whiteouts"
.TP
.B The Problem
When a user\-installed package is removed from the overlay:
.RS
.IP 1. 3
User installs a package (e.g., SDL) into the overlay
.IP 2.
New RAUC update integrates that package into the base image
.IP 3.
During reconciliation,
.B opkg remove
deletes the overlay package
.IP 4.
OverlayFS creates whiteout files (character device 0:0) for files that exist in base image
.IP 5.
Whiteouts persist, blocking access to newer base image version
.RE
.TP
.B The Solution
The reconciliation system automatically removes package file whiteouts:
.RS
.IP 1. 3
Pre\-fetches file list for each package
.B before
calling
.B opkg remove
(removal deletes package from database)
.IP 2.
After removal, asks overlayfs (OVL_IOC_UPPER_STATE) which of those paths are whiteouts
.IP 3.
Removes them with OVL_IOC_RESTORE_LOWER, which also refreshes the overlay's caches, so no remount is needed
.RE
.SS "Metadata Whiteouts"
.TP
.B The Problem
When
.B opkg remove
deletes metadata files in
.IR /var/lib/opkg/info/ :
.RS
.IP 1. 3
Package removal deletes files like
.IR package.list ,
.IR package.control
.IP 2.
OverlayFS creates
.I .wh.package.list
whiteout files
.IP 3.
Whiteouts hide base image's metadata files
.IP 4.
Commands like
.B opkg files package
fail even though package exists in base
.RE
.TP
.B The Solution
After package removal, the system:
.RS
.IP 1. 3
Checks the package's
.IR /var/lib/opkg/info/ package .*
paths with OVL_IOC_UPPER_STATE
.IP 2.
Removes the whiteouts with OVL_IOC_RESTORE_LOWER to expose the base image's metadata
.IP 3.
Allows opkg queries to work correctly
.RE
.PP
The ioctls come from Calculinux's overlayfs module (https://github.com/Calculinux/overlayfs); a kernel without them makes the hook fail rather than guess.
.SS "Enhanced opkg Integration"
The system uses enhanced opkg functionality for proper split\-status file handling:
.TP
.B opkg status \-\-writable\-only
Queries only the writable status file (/var/lib/opkg/status), ignoring base image packages. Used to verify package removal from overlay.
.TP
.B opkg status \-\-image\-only
Queries only the base image status file (/var/lib/opkg/status.image), ignoring overlay packages.
.TP
.B opkg status \-\-show\-source
Shows which status file(s) contain the package (writable, image, or both).
.PP
These flags eliminate manual status file parsing and provide proper API for dual\-layer package management.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/calculinux\-update.toml
Main configuration file defining channels and mirror settings.
.TP
.I /var/cache/calculinux\-update/
Cache directory for downloaded bundles and prefetched packages.
.TP
.I /var/cache/calculinux\-update/prefetch/
Prefetched .ipk package files for offline reconciliation.
.TP
.I /var/lib/opkg/status
Current installed package status (writable overlay).
.TP
.I /var/lib/opkg/status.image
Base image package status (read\-only, provided by base image).
.TP
.I /var/lib/calculinux\-update/update\-state.pending\-reinstalls
Overlay packages queued for reinstall (created by cup\-hook; emptied by cup\-postreboot or cup reconcile).
.TP
.I /var/lib/calculinux\-update/update\-state.leftovers
Packages that could not be reinstalled (shown by cup status).
.TP
.I /var/lib/calculinux\-update/prefetch.json
What the prefetch cache holds and which image it was made for.
.TP
.I /var/lib/calculinux\-update/prefetch\-lists/
Feed lists of the new image, used by the post\-reboot reinstall.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/cup\-reconcile.timer
Retries queued reinstalls every 15 minutes while any are waiting.
.TP
.I /etc/profile.d/cup\-pending.sh
Login message listing waiting reinstalls and how to finish them.
.TP
.I /var/lib/calculinux\-update/update\-state.pending\-duplicates
Physical duplicate packages queued for removal after reboot (created by cup\-hook). These are packages with actual files in the upper layer that must be removed after switching to the new slot.
.TP
.I /var/lib/calculinux\-update/update\-state.modified\-conffiles
List of config files modified by user that shadow new versions from updated image (created by cup\-hook). Each line contains JSON with
.I path
and
.I package
fields. Used by
.B cup\-postreboot
to report files needing manual review.
.TP
.I /var/lib/calculinux\-update/status\-pruned
Marker file indicating cup\-hook completed status pruning.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/cup\-postreboot.service
Systemd service that runs
.B cup\-postreboot
on first boot after update (triggered by pending files or absence of status\-pruned marker).
.TP
.I /usr/lib/rauc/post\-install\-handler.sh
RAUC post\-install handler that calls
.BR cup\-hook .
.TP
.I /usr/lib/calculinux\-update/cup\-hook
RAUC hook binary for reconciliation planning (invoked by post\-install handler).
.TP
.I /usr/lib/calculinux\-update/cup\-postreboot
Post\-reboot reconciliation binary (invoked by systemd service).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LOGLEVEL
Set logging verbosity. Valid values:
.BR DEBUG ", " INFO ", " WARNING ", " ERROR .
Default is
.BR INFO .
.PP
Example:
.PP
.nf
.RS
LOGLEVEL=DEBUG cup install
.RE
.fi
.TP
.B RAUC_BUNDLE_STATUS_IMAGE
(Hook only) Path to status.image file from bundle extras (e.g.,
.IR /tmp/rauc-bundle/extras/opkg/status.image
). Provided by RAUC post\-install handler, allows hook to access new image's package status without mounting the slot.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS "Interactive Update"
Install the latest update interactively:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo cup install
.RE
.fi
.PP
This lists available channels, bundles, prefetches packages, and prompts for confirmation.
.SS "Update from Specific Channel"
Install latest from Release channel:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo cup install \-\-channel "Release"
.RE
.fi
.SS "Install Specific Bundle"
Install a known bundle without prompts:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo cup install \-\-bundle calculinux\-bundle\-20241120.raucb \-\-yes
.RE
.fi
.SS "List Available Updates"
Show bundles from all enabled channels:
.PP
.nf
.RS
cup list
.RE
.fi
.PP
Show bundles from specific channel:
.PP
.nf
.RS
cup list \-\-channel "Continuous"
.RE
.fi
.SS "Download Without Installing"
Download a bundle for offline installation:
.PP
.nf
.RS
cup download \-\-bundle calculinux\-bundle\-20241120.raucb
.RE
.fi
.PP
Later install from cache:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo rauc install /var/cache/calculinux\-update/calculinux\-bundle\-20241120.raucb
.RE
.fi
.SS "Development Machine Update"
Skip prefetch on machines without OPKG configured:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo cup install \-\-no\-prefetch
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B Warning:
Network access required after reboot for reconciliation.
.SS "Check Reconciliation Status"
After update and reboot, check reconciliation logs:
.PP
.nf
.RS
journalctl \-u calculinux\-update\-postreboot.service
.RE
.fi
.PP
Look for messages like:
.PP
.nf
.RS
cup\-hook: identified 2 status\-only duplicates (safe to remove now)
cup\-hook: identified 3 physical duplicates (deferred until reboot)
cup\-hook: removed 2 status\-only duplicate packages
cup\-hook: removed 15 package whiteout files
cup\-hook: removed 8 metadata whiteout files
cup\-hook: queued 3 physical duplicates for post\-reboot removal
cup\-hook: release change: 4 overlay package(s) will be reinstalled
cup\-hook: queued 4 packages for reinstall
cup\-hook: detected 2 modified config files
cup\-hook: marked status as pruned for new image
cup\-postreboot: removing 3 physical duplicate packages
cup\-postreboot: removed 12 whiteout files
cup\-postreboot: reinstalling 4 package(s) from the prefetch cache
cup\-postreboot: reconciliation complete

Modified config files detected (user changes shadow new image versions):
Package: openssh\-server
  /etc/ssh/sshd_config \-> /etc/ssh/sshd_config.dpkg\-new
Package: busybox
  /etc/busybox.conf \-> /etc/busybox.conf.dpkg\-new
.RE
.fi
.SS "Manual Hook Invocation"
Manually run hook (for debugging, requires bundle extras):
.PP
.nf
.RS
export RAUC_BUNDLE_STATUS_IMAGE=/path/to/status.image
export RAUC_SLOT_CLASS=rootfs
sudo cup\-hook slot\-post\-install rootfs.1
.RE
.fi
.SS "Manual Post\-Reboot Reconciliation"
If post\-reboot service failed, retry manually:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo cup\-postreboot
.RE
.fi
.PP
Or via systemd:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo systemctl restart calculinux\-update\-postreboot.service
.RE
.fi
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.SS "Exit Status"
.TP
.B 0
Success. Operation completed without errors.
.TP
.B 1
Failure. Check logs for details.
.SS "Logging"
All commands log to stderr. Set
.B LOGLEVEL=DEBUG
for detailed diagnostics.
.SS "Common Issues"
.TP
.B "Update fails to install"
Check RAUC status:
.PP
.nf
.RS
rauc status
journalctl \-u rauc \-e
.RE
.fi
.TP
.B "Packages not reconciling after reboot"
Check post\-reboot service:
.PP
.nf
.RS
systemctl status cup\-postreboot.service
journalctl \-u cup\-postreboot.service \-e
.RE
.fi
.PP
Pending operations are stored in:
.PP
.nf
.RS
/var/lib/calculinux\-update/update\-state.pending\-reinstalls
/var/lib/calculinux\-update/update\-state.pending\-duplicates
.RE
.fi
.TP
.B "Modified config files after update"
Check for modified config files reported by post\-reboot service:
.PP
.nf
.RS
journalctl \-u cup\-postreboot.service | grep "Modified config files"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Or check the state file directly:
.PP
.nf
.RS
cat /var/lib/calculinux\-update/update\-state.modified\-conffiles
.RE
.fi
.PP
Review and merge changes:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# List all .dpkg\-new files
find /etc /usr /var \-name "*.dpkg\-new"

# Compare changes
diff /etc/ssh/sshd_config /etc/ssh/sshd_config.dpkg\-new

# Use interactive merge tool
vimdiff /etc/ssh/sshd_config /etc/ssh/sshd_config.dpkg\-new
.RE
.fi
.PP
After resolving conflicts, remove
.I .dpkg\-new
files or keep them for reference.
.TP
.B "Base image files not accessible after update"
Whiteout files may be blocking access. Check for character device 0:0 files:
.PP
.nf
.RS
find /usr /etc /var \-type c \-exec stat {} \\; | grep "Device type: 0,0"
.RE
.fi
.PP
If whiteout cleanup failed during update, it can be retriggered by the post\-reboot service. Check logs:
.PP
.nf
.RS
journalctl \-u cup\-postreboot.service | grep whiteout
.RE
.fi
.PP
Metadata whiteouts in /var/lib/opkg/info/ prevent package queries:
.PP
.nf
.RS
ls \-la /var/lib/opkg/info/.wh.*
.RE
.fi
.PP
These should be removed automatically. If they persist, report a bug.
.TP
.B "Prefetch fails"
OPKG may not be configured, or bundle extras may be missing. Check for bundle extras:
.PP
.nf
.RS
unsquashfs \-ll bundle.raucb | grep bundle\-extras.tar.gz
.RE
.fi
.PP
If bundle extras are missing, use
.BR \-\-no\-prefetch :
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo cup install \-\-no\-prefetch
.RE
.fi
.PP
Ensure network access is available after reboot.
.TP
.B "Bundle extras corrupted"
If bundle extras extraction fails, the bundle may be corrupted. Re\-download:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo cup download \-\-bundle <bundle\-name>
sudo cup install \-\-bundle <bundle\-name>
.RE
.fi
.TP
.B "Network unavailable after reboot"
If prefetch succeeded, reinstalls work offline from
.IR /var/cache/calculinux\-update/prefetch/ .
Anything else waits; the login message and
.B cup status
list it. Connect to the network, then run:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo cup reconcile
.RE
.fi
.SH ROLLBACK
To roll back to the previous slot:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo rauc status mark-bad booted
sudo reboot
.RE
.fi
.PP
Or select the other slot in your bootloader (if supported).
